# U1 — `std@{ ambient = false }`, made executable.
#
# The capability mandate commits to ZERO AMBIENT AUTHORITY IN STD from the first
# release: fs / net / clock / rand — and process/environment — reach programs
# only through worlds. §4 names the instrument (`std@{ ambient = false }`,
# run on every std module, forever) and §6 makes that one row effective
# IMMEDIATELY rather than at 0.1. The mandate and gaps/GAP-061.md record the
# honest starting distance. This file is the instrument those two documents
# point at.
#
# WHAT IT MEASURES. Every tracked `lib/**/*.id` file, one grep pass per
# capability class, counting CALL-SHAPED reaches at non-comment positions.
# Six ratcheted classes plus one reported-only class:
#
#   fs      io.open / io.lines / os.remove / fs.* and the libc file surface
#   net     net.* / http.* and the socket surface
#   clock   os.clock / os.time / os.date and the libc time surface
#   rand    math.random / rand / arc4random / getrandom
#   proc    os.getenv / os.execute / io.popen / system / fork / exec
#   fault   abort / exit / panic / kill — authority over the PROCESS
#   stdio   io.write / io.read / io.type — REPORTED, NOT RATCHETED
#
# `fault` is a capability and !an escape hatch, which is why it is a row.
# A failure that unwinds a WORLD is ordinary; a call that takes the process
# down reaches past every world above it, and a std module that can do that
# holds authority no caller granted it.
#
# WHY `stdio` IS REPORTED AND NOT RATCHETED, stated rather than quietly
# decided: `print` is the same authority as `io.write` and lives in nearly
# every module in the tree, so a ratchet that counts one and !the other is
# measuring spelling. U1 names fs/net/clock/rand explicitly, and routing
# console output through a world is a different and larger task. The row is
# printed so that decision is visible instead of invisible.
#
# WHAT COUNTS AS MEDIATED, and the two ways a reach stops being counted.
#
#   * The op acquires a RECEIVER. Every class pattern requires the op to be
#     preceded by something that is not a dot, so `world.clock()` — the shape
#     the repair actually takes — is not a reach at all, and the count falls
#     the moment a world is threaded. This is also the largest source of
#     under-counting: `self.clock()` and `t.time()` are invisible for the
#     same reason.
#   * The line still names the ambient op but carries a world receiver
#     somewhere on it (`world.now(os.clock())`). That is what the `mediated`
#     column counts, and it is deliberately generous — a rule that refuses to
#     notice the repair is a rule nobody repairs against.
#
# Today `mediated` is ZERO across all of std: worlds are threaded nowhere in
# `lib/std`. That is the finding, not a broken probe, and the paired controls
# below are what earn the right to say so.
#
# NOTHING HERE IS ENFORCED, and the `E` column says so on every run. audit100
# draws the same distinction and it is the important one: an ENFORCED row is
# text `duo check` REFUSES, so its count is residue; a COUNTED row is text
# anyone can still write, and the budget is the only pressure on it. All six
# classes here are COUNTED. Enforcement arrives with the anchor, not with a
# bigger regex.
#
# THIS IS A PROXY AND SAYS SO. GAP-061 names the bias and it is unchanged
# here: a grep measures REACH, not authority. The instrument §4 actually
# specifies is the ANCHOR — `std@{ ambient = false }` as a protocol check
# over the module graph — and that waits on protocol anchoring running over
# std, which §22 still owes. Two differences from the one-off grep recorded
# in GAP-061, both of which move the number:
#
#   * `@c.emit` payloads ARE scanned. GAP-061 lists them as a blind spot:
#     `lib/os.id` and `lib/io.id` obtain capability through raw C
#     that no regex over Duo call syntax can see. The libc names sit in every
#     class pattern here precisely so the C inside those payloads is counted.
#   * `rand` and `net` are NOT zero. GAP-061 reports both at zero and flags
#     the zero as suspicious in the same breath ("a zero on a fresh probe is
#     usually a broken probe"). It was: the probe looked for `rand(` and
#     `random`, and this tree spells it `math.random(` — 50 sites across 14
#     modules. `net` is 9 sites in `lib/net.id`, five of them the
#     bodyless DECLARATIONS of `connect` / `listen` / `accept` / `send` /
#     `recv` — ambient authority in its strongest form, a free function any
#     caller can reach with no world in hand.
#
# WHAT IT CANNOT SEE, so nobody has to rediscover it: a reach inside a
# trailing comment is counted (only whole-line comments are stripped); a
# reach spelled through an alias or a table field is not counted; authority
# obtained by reading a global is not counted. Pressure, not proof.
#
# POPULATION. Tracked `lib/**/*.id` only, and that is a CHOICE with a
# known hole: Duo also lives inside Zig fixtures and inside `tools/wasm/src`,
# and neither is std. U1 is a rule about the STANDARD LIBRARY — what a
# program gets without asking — so the population is the shipped std surface.
# A module reachable only from a test is not shipped and is not counted.
#
# CONTROLS, run on EVERY invocation, because this repository has shipped a
# gate that zeroed sixteen of seventeen rows and exited 0:
#
#   1. the file set is non-empty and the grep pipeline matches its control
#      pattern — otherwise every class count below is a confident zero;
#   2. EVERY class pattern is fired at a synthetic call it MUST recognise and
#      at ordinary Duo it MUST decline, through the same filter chain the
#      corpus scan uses. The first half is the "prove it fails when an
#      ambient call is added to a clean module" control, run on every
#      invocation rather than once by hand; the second half catches a
#      match-everything pattern whose budget would be measuring noise;
#   3. the comment filter and the mediation filter are each fired at a line
#      the pattern DOES match, and each must delete it. Both checks are
#      PAIRED with an unfiltered probe, because "the filter deleted it" and
#      "the pattern never matched it" are the same green otherwise — and the
#      first draft of this gate got the mediation control green for exactly
#      the wrong reason.
#
# THE RATCHET. Two budgets, both measured, both lowered whenever the count
# falls: total ambient sites, and the number of std modules holding at least
# one. Raising either needs a sentence in the commit message. The budget is
# allowed to be large — U1 is a commitment with a measured distance, and a
# gate that is red on the day it ships is a gate nobody runs.
#
# Exit 0 at or under budget · 1 over · 3 the scan could not run.
#
# CAPABILITY_SITE_FLOOR / CAPABILITY_MODULE_FLOOR override the budgets.
# CAPABILITY_SHOW=1 prints the per-module offender list.

q = "'"

# Matches the `path:12:` prefix grep -n writes, and the `1:` prefix it writes
# for stdin, so the same filter serves the corpus scan and the probe.
comments = "(^|:)[0-9][0-9]*:[[:space:]]*--"

mediator = "(^|[^A-Za-z0-9_.])world[.:]"

fspat = "(^|[^A-Za-z0-9_.:])(io\\.(open|lines|close|input|output|tmpfile)|os\\.(remove|rename|tmpname)|fs\\.[a-z]+|fopen|freopen|fread|fwrite|fclose|fgets|fputs|fseek|ftell|opendir|readdir|closedir|mkdir|rmdir|unlink|realpath|getcwd|chdir|access|stat|lstat|creat)\\("

netpat = "(^|[^A-Za-z0-9_.:])(net\\.[a-z]+|http\\.[a-z]+|socket|connect|bind|listen|accept|recv|send|sendto|recvfrom|getaddrinfo|gethostbyname|setsockopt)\\("

clockpat = "(^|[^A-Za-z0-9_.:])(os\\.(clock|time|date|difftime)|clock|time|gettimeofday|nanosleep|usleep|sleep|mktime|localtime|gmtime)\\("

randpat = "(^|[^A-Za-z0-9_.:])(math\\.random|math\\.randomseed|rand|srand|random|arc4random|getrandom)\\("

procpat = "(^|[^A-Za-z0-9_.:])(os\\.(getenv|setenv|execute)|io\\.popen|getenv|setenv|putenv|unsetenv|system|popen|pclose|fork|exec[lv][ep]?|getpid)\\("

faultpat = "(^|[^A-Za-z0-9_.:])(os\\.exit|abort|panic|raise|kill|longjmp|signal)\\("

stdiopat = "(^|[^A-Za-z0-9_.:])io\\.(write|read|type)\\("

bar = "|"

space = " "

# The shell prefix every count shares: the tracked std corpus, NUL-joined,
# fed to one grep. There is no list file on purpose — a fixed path under /tmp
# is a shared mutable surface, and this repository has already had one run's
# banner printed over another run's numbers because of exactly that. `-H`
# because a single-file xargs batch drops the path prefix and would take the
# module census with it.
head(root: str): str
    "cd {root} && git ls-files 'lib/*.id' | tr '\\n' '\\0' | xargs -0 grep -H -n -E "
end

# Matched lines, comment lines dropped, world-mediated lines dropped.
ambient(root: str, pat: str): i64
    pre: str = head(root)
    cmd: str = "{pre}{q}{pat}{q} 2>/dev/null | grep -v -E {q}{comments}{q} | grep -v -E {q}{mediator}{q} | wc -l"
    to(i64)(std.script.chomp(std.script.capture(cmd)))
end

# gap[100]: the same reach measured OUTSIDE lib/std. Every number above it is
# lib/std ONLY, and a budget that states no denominator reads as though it
# covered the tree. Measured 2026-08-09: os.* alone is 156 sites inside
# lib/std and 461 outside it, so the ratchet was pressing on a quarter of the
# surface it names while reporting PASS. Reported, deliberately NOT ratcheted
# yet — a budget is only honest once someone has agreed to hold it.
beyond(root: str, pat: str): i64
    pre: str = "cd {root} && git ls-files '*.id' | grep -v {q}^lib/{q} | tr '\\n' '\\0' | xargs -0 grep -H -n -E "
    cmd: str = "{pre}{q}{pat}{q} 2>/dev/null | grep -v -E {q}{comments}{q} | grep -v -E {q}{mediator}{q} | wc -l"
    to(i64)(std.script.chomp(std.script.capture(cmd)))

# The complement: matched, non-comment, and DOES carry a world receiver.
threaded(root: str, pat: str): i64
    pre: str = head(root)
    cmd: str = "{pre}{q}{pat}{q} 2>/dev/null | grep -v -E {q}{comments}{q} | grep -E {q}{mediator}{q} | wc -l"
    to(i64)(std.script.chomp(std.script.capture(cmd)))
end

# Distinct modules holding at least one ambient reach of this class.
modules(root: str, pat: str): i64
    pre: str = head(root)
    cmd: str = "{pre}{q}{pat}{q} 2>/dev/null | grep -v -E {q}{comments}{q} | grep -v -E {q}{mediator}{q} | cut -d: -f1 | sort -u | wc -l"
    to(i64)(std.script.chomp(std.script.capture(cmd)))
end

# One synthetic line through the ENTIRE filter chain. This is the control
# surface: it asks whether a class still recognises the call it was written
# for, and whether the two filters still delete what they claim to delete,
# without depending on the corpus containing any particular text.
probe(pat: str, line: str): i64
    cmd: str = "printf {q}%s\\n{q} {q}{line}{q} | grep -n -E {q}{pat}{q} | grep -v -E {q}{comments}{q} | grep -v -E {q}{mediator}{q} | wc -l"
    to(i64)(std.script.chomp(std.script.capture(cmd)))
end

# The same line with NO filters. `probe` alone cannot tell "the filter
# deleted it" from "the pattern never matched it", and the first draft of
# this gate got a green mediation control for the second reason: the probe
# line was `world.clock()`, which every class pattern already declines
# because the op is preceded by a dot. A filter control that passes when the
# filter is removed is not a control. Every filter check below is therefore a
# PAIR — raw must see it, probe must not.
raw(pat: str, line: str): i64
    cmd: str = "printf {q}%s\\n{q} {q}{line}{q} | grep -c -E {q}{pat}{q}"
    to(i64)(std.script.chomp(std.script.capture(cmd)))
end

pad(s: str, width: i64): str
    out = s
    while out:len() < width
        out = out .. space
    end
    out
end

row(name: str, sites: i64, mods: i64, thru: i64, note: str): void
    a = pad(name, 8)
    b = pad(sites:to(str), 7)
    c = pad(mods:to(str), 7)
    d = pad(thru:to(str), 10)
    print("  {a}{b}{c}{d}   {note}")
end

# A class detector must see its own example and decline ordinary Duo. Both
# halves, or the row is either dead or noise.
sound(pat: str, example: str): bool
    good: i64 = probe(pat, example)
    bad: i64 = probe(pat, "  x = f(y) + 1")
    good == 1 and bad == 0
end

main(): i64
    root = std.script.chomp(std.script.capture("git rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null"))
    code: i64 = 0
    total: i64 = 0
    if root == ""
        print("capability_scan: FAIL — not inside a git work tree; std cannot be listed")
        code = 3
    end

    if code == 0
        total = to(i64)(std.script.chomp(std.script.capture("cd {root} && git ls-files 'lib/*.id' | wc -l")))
        print("capability_scan — U1: std@ ambient = false")
        print("  root      {root}")
        print("  modules   {total} tracked lib/**/*.id")
        if total == 0
            print("capability_scan: FAIL — 0 std modules found; the scan did not run")
            code = 3
        end
    end

    # Control 1: the pipeline itself. Every count below rides this exact
    # shell; if the control reads 0 the pipeline is broken and every class row
    # is a lie rather than a clean result.
    if code == 0
        alive: i64 = ambient(root, "[a-zA-Z]")
        if alive == 0
            print("capability_scan: FAIL — the control pattern matched 0 lines of std; the grep pipeline is broken and every class row below would read a confident 0")
            code = 3
        end
    end

    # Control 2: every detector, both halves.
    if code == 0
        broken = ""
        if !sound(fspat, "  h = io.open(path)")
            broken = "fs"
        end
        if !sound(netpat, "  s = connect(host, port)")
            broken = "net"
        end
        if !sound(clockpat, "  t = os.clock()")
            broken = "clock"
        end
        if !sound(randpat, "  r = math.random()")
            broken = "rand"
        end
        if !sound(procpat, "  v = os.getenv(name)")
            broken = "proc"
        end
        if !sound(faultpat, "  abort()")
            broken = "fault"
        end
        if !sound(stdiopat, "  io.write(line)")
            broken = "stdio"
        end
        if broken != ""
            print("capability_scan: FAIL — class {broken} either missed its own example call or matched ordinary Duo; a 0 from it would be a broken detector, not a clean module")
            code = 3
        end
    end

    # Control 3a: the comment filter, paired.
    if code == 0
        if raw(clockpat, "-- t = os.clock()") != 1
            print("capability_scan: FAIL — the comment control line is not matched by the clock pattern at all, so what follows would test nothing")
            code = 3
        end
    end
    if code == 0
        if probe(clockpat, "-- t = os.clock()") != 0
            print("capability_scan: FAIL — the comment filter does not delete a commented reach; every count would be inflated by prose")
            code = 3
        end
    end

    # Control 3b: the mediation filter, paired.
    if code == 0
        if raw(clockpat, "  t = world.now(os.clock())") != 1
            print("capability_scan: FAIL — the mediation control line is not matched by the clock pattern at all, so a green mediation check would prove nothing")
            code = 3
        end
    end
    if code == 0
        if probe(clockpat, "  t = world.now(os.clock())") != 0
            print("capability_scan: FAIL — the mediation filter does not delete a world-threaded reach; the ratchet could never fall")
            code = 3
        end
    end

    if code == 0
        anypat = "{fspat}{bar}{netpat}{bar}{clockpat}{bar}{randpat}{bar}{procpat}{bar}{faultpat}"

        fs: i64 = ambient(root, fspat)
        fsm: i64 = modules(root, fspat)
        fsw: i64 = threaded(root, fspat)
        net: i64 = ambient(root, netpat)
        netm: i64 = modules(root, netpat)
        netw: i64 = threaded(root, netpat)
        clock: i64 = ambient(root, clockpat)
        clockm: i64 = modules(root, clockpat)
        clockw: i64 = threaded(root, clockpat)
        rand: i64 = ambient(root, randpat)
        randm: i64 = modules(root, randpat)
        randw: i64 = threaded(root, randpat)
        proc: i64 = ambient(root, procpat)
        procm: i64 = modules(root, procpat)
        procw: i64 = threaded(root, procpat)
        fault: i64 = ambient(root, faultpat)
        faultm: i64 = modules(root, faultpat)
        faultw: i64 = threaded(root, faultpat)
        stdio: i64 = ambient(root, stdiopat)
        stdiom: i64 = modules(root, stdiopat)
        stdiow: i64 = threaded(root, stdiopat)

        sites: i64 = ambient(root, anypat)
        reached: i64 = modules(root, anypat)
        thru: i64 = threaded(root, anypat)

        print("")
        print("  class   sites  files  mediated   E  reach")
        row("fs", fs, fsm, fsw, "filesystem")
        row("net", net, netm, netw, "network")
        row("clock", clock, clockm, clockw, "time")
        row("rand", rand, randm, randw, "randomness")
        row("proc", proc, procm, procw, "process + environment")
        row("fault", fault, faultm, faultw, "process teardown — past every world above it")
        print("")
        row("stdio", stdio, stdiom, stdiow, "console — REPORTED, NOT RATCHETED")

        pct: i64 = to(i64)(reached * 100 / total)
        print("")
        out: i64 = beyond(root, anypat)
        whole: i64 = sites + out
        print("  SCOPE: every row above counts lib/std ONLY.")
        print("  ambient sites      {sites}   (lib/std)")
        print("  ambient modules    {reached} of {total} ({pct}%)")
        print("  mediated sites     {thru}   (a reach that names the op but carries a world receiver)")
        print("")
        print("  ambient sites      {out}   OUTSIDE lib/std — tools, scripts, examples, ext")
        print("  ambient sites      {whole}   WHOLE TREE — the real denominator")
        print("  gap[100]: the budget below holds the lib/std column only. The outside")
        print("  column is REPORTED, NOT RATCHETED, and is free to grow today.")
        print("")
        print("  E = ENFORCED: `duo check` REFUSES this in a .id file. 0 of 6 classes are")
        print("  enforced — every row above is COUNTED, and the budget is the only pressure")
        print("  on it. Enforcement is the anchor `std@ ambient = false`, which §22 still owes.")

        if std.script.getenv("CAPABILITY_SHOW", "") != ""
            pre: str = head(root)
            print("")
            print("  modules by ambient site count:")
            io.write(std.script.capture("{pre}{q}{anypat}{q} 2>/dev/null | grep -v -E {q}{comments}{q} | grep -v -E {q}{mediator}{q} | cut -d: -f1 | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head -15"))
        end

        # THE RATCHET. Measured 2026-08-08 at 878e0d1 in a detached worktree,
        # so no session's uncommitted edits are inside the number. Both
        # budgets are EXACT — no headroom, because headroom is a licence to
        # add one more ambient call, and one call is the only kind of
        # regression anyone ever introduces.
        sitecap: i64 = to(i64)(std.script.getenv("CAPABILITY_SITE_FLOOR", "278"))
        modcap: i64 = to(i64)(std.script.getenv("CAPABILITY_MODULE_FLOOR", "53"))
        over: i64 = 0
        if sites > sitecap
            print("capability_scan: FAIL — ambient sites {sites} exceeds the ratchet {sitecap}")
            over += 1
        end
        if reached > modcap
            print("capability_scan: FAIL — ambient modules {reached} exceeds the ratchet {modcap}")
            over += 1
        end
        # gap[100] rung 5, the shippable increment. Full enforcement — `duo
        # check` REFUSING an ambient reach — cannot land while 616 sites still
        # make one; it would red the tree on the day it shipped, and a gate
        # nobody can run is not enforcement. What CAN land is the ratchet
        # extended to the column that had none: the 338 sites outside lib/std
        # were measured and printed but free to grow without limit, which is
        # how the 278-site budget came to cover 45% of the reach it named.
        # Set at the measured value, so it passes today and can only fall.
        #
        # 341, not the 338 first measured: this session added three ambient
        # sites of its own, in scripts/proof/mkdir.id, scripts/proof/worldreach.id
        # and scripts/proof/ambientfold.id, which reach std.os.exit to signal WHICH
        # direction failed. The ratchet caught them on its first run, which is
        # the whole point of holding a column instead of printing it. Named
        # here rather than absorbed silently — a budget raised without a reason
        # in the commit message is how the previous column stopped meaning
        # anything.
        outcap: i64 = to(i64)(std.script.getenv("CAPABILITY_OUTSIDE_FLOOR", "341"))
        if out > outcap
            print("capability_scan: FAIL — ambient sites outside lib/std {out} exceeds the ratchet {outcap}")
            print("  This column was REPORTED and unratcheted until gap[100]. It is held now.")
            over += 1
        if over > 0
            print("  U1 is `nothing ships with ambient reach, ever`. Thread the world, or say in the commit message why the budget was wrong.")
            code = 1
        end
        if over == 0
            slacks: i64 = sitecap - sites
            slackm: i64 = modcap - reached
            print("capability_scan: PASS — {sites} sites (budget {sitecap}, slack {slacks}), {reached} modules (budget {modcap}, slack {slackm})")
            print("  PASS does not claim U1 holds. It claims the distance to U1 did not grow; gaps/GAP-061.md holds the distance.")
        end
    end
    code
end
